  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nStates a new approach that leverages the combinatorial structure of optimum solution to Erds problem and applies iterative refinement on a larger grid (n=560) to improve resolution and candidate exploration. This method ensures continuous progress by maintaining pipeline efficiency and using multiobjective optimization via Pareto ranking. The active-set strategy from the parent is adapted but with prioritized lag resolution. Unlike the previous algorithm that used stepwise phases, the new plan merges optimization methods to ensure convergence.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nFormulates the optimization as:  \nMinimize: C = (2/n)*max_k[ np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') ]  \nSubject to: sum(h)=n/2 and 0\u2264h[i]\u22641  \n- h represented as complex step function with real values  \n- Target: C=0.380859  \n- Formulation allows convex iterative steps via multi-primal reformulations  \n\n3. Implementation procedure\nRuns:  \nA. Initialization: set parent/n_points (initial n=560)  \nB. Objective function: adapted Erd\u0151s overlap integral via correlation with clipping  \nC. Active-set selection: prioritize top-3 lags from the right tail  \nD. Barrier optimization: use decreasing T in SLSQP  \nE. Smoothed polishing: exponential learning rate via coordinate descent  \n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Base configuration uses 560 points (implicit doubling)\n- Allows dimension adjustment by linear interpolation  \n- Key parameters: ${T_min}=0.05$, ${T_max}=25600  \n- Variation \"${n_size}\" supports sizes [560, 2240]  \n\n5. Budget and robustness\nEstimates ~35-40ms per barrier iteration for n=560  \nExpects ~40K iterations in budget window  \nSafety: halts on NaN/All-Increase convergence  \n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFinal solution undergoes clipping/repair  \nMust satisfy: sum(h)%dx=1.0 and 0<=h[i]<=1  \nComputes final C value with `compute_c5`  \nIncludes fallback mechanism for hilloft optimization failure",
